Welcome to the Slack kit. This kit is designed to help you understand, query, and make the most of the audit logs generated by Slack.

This playbook serves to get you oriented around the kit and provide some anchors into the data and dashboards.

# Getting Data In

Slack exposes the organization audit log through the [Audit Logs API](https://docs.slack.dev/admins/audit-logs-api) at `GET /api/v1/audit/v1/logs`. The API requires an **Enterprise Grid** plan and an OAuth token holding the `auditlogs:read` scope, issued to an org-level app installed by an Org Owner.

The response contains an `entries` array. Poll it on a schedule (use the `oldest`/`latest` bounds and follow the `response_metadata.next_cursor` for pagination), and ingest **each element of the array as its own entry** into the `slack-audit` tag. A Gravwell flow, or any JSON-capable poller against the HTTP ingester, works.

**Timestamps:** `date_create` is a Unix epoch integer in seconds, not an ISO-8601 string. Configure the ingest timestamp to read that field, or the entries will be stamped with their arrival time instead of when the action occurred.

# Overview

Every Slack audit entry has the same top-level shape: **what happened** (`action`), **who did it** (`actor`), **what it happened to** (`entity`), and **where it happened from** (`context`). A `details` object carries action-specific extras.

```
{
  "id": "0123a45b-6c7d-8900-e12f-3456789gh0i1",
  "date_create": 1521214343,
  "action": "user_login",
  "actor":   { "type": "user", "user": { "id": "W123AB456", "name": "Charlie Parker", "email": "bront@slack-corp.com" } },
  "entity":  { "type": "user", "user": { "id": "W123AB456", "name": "Charlie Parker", "email": "bront@slack-corp.com" } },
  "context": { "location": { "type": "enterprise", "id": "E1701NCCA", "name": "Enterprise", "domain": "yoyodyne" },
               "ua": "Mozilla/5.0 ...", "ip_address": "1.23.45.678" }
}
```

Note that `actor` and `entity` are **both** frequently users, and on a self-directed action such as `user_login` they are the same person. On an administrative action they are not: `actor` is who made the change, `entity` is who it was made to.

## Field naming in this kit

Slack nests everything, and two different nested paths - `actor.user.name` and `entity.user.name` - would otherwise collapse to the same field name. The kit's autoextractor therefore assigns every nested path an explicit, unambiguous name:

| Slack path | Field name in this kit |
|---|---|
| `actor.user.name` / `actor.user.email` | `actorName` / `actorEmail` |
| `entity.user.name` / `entity.user.email` | `targetUserName` / `targetUserEmail` |
| `entity.channel.name` | `channelName` |
| `entity.file.name` | `fileName` |
| `entity.app.name` / `entity.app.scopes` | `appName` / `appScopes` |
| `context.ip_address` / `context.ua` | `ipAddress` / `userAgent` |
| `context.location.name` | `locationName` |
| `details.previous_value` / `details.new_value` | `detailPreviousValue` / `detailNewValue` |

Queries reference those names through the `ax` module:

```
tag=$SLACK_AUDIT ax action=="user_login_failed" actorName
| count by actorName over 10m
| eval count > 5
| table -nt actorName count
```

`appScopes` is a JSON array and renders as `["channels:read","files:write"]` in tables. To expand it into one entry per scope, append `| json -x appScopes`. Note that `-x` takes the name of an already-extracted field, so it wants the autoextractor's `appScopes` name here, not the raw `entity.app.scopes` path.

## Actions

Slack defines several hundred `action` values, documented in the [audit logs actions reference](https://docs.slack.dev/reference/audit-logs-api/methods-actions-reference/). The detections in this kit target the security-relevant subset. Two conventions are worth knowing when you write your own queries:

- Workspace and organization preference changes are prefixed `pref.` (for example `pref.two_factor_auth_changed`), and carry the old and new settings in `details.previous_value` and `details.new_value`.
- Actions are past-tense and specific - `file_public_link_created`, not `file_shared`. Match them exactly rather than with substrings; `in(action, "a", "b")` is exact-match and is what the kit uses.

# Queries
Queries are broken out into 2 types and have appropriate labels applied for reference.

## Stats
Primarily used by dashboards to generate metrics and provide an overview of activity across the Slack organization.

## Detections
Queries built to detect specific behavior that can be indicative of malicious activity: failed login spikes, logins from multiple addresses, admin and owner role grants, authentication policy and IdP changes, app installation and scope expansion, public file links, bulk downloads, malicious file content, workspace exports, external Slack Connect channels, removal of DLP and legal hold controls, EKM changes, Slack-flagged anomalies, and audit log access. These can be used ad hoc, serve as a basis for your own detection engineering, or enabled as scheduled searches for alerting. Threshold-based detections should be tuned to your environment.

# Scheduled Searches
Every detection ships with a matching scheduled search that runs hourly over the previous two hours of data. The overlapping window accounts for API polling delay, so a threshold detection may alert twice for the same burst. They are **disabled by default** - review the thresholds, then enable the ones you want from the Scheduled Searches page.

# Dashboards
3 dashboards are provided in this kit.

## Slack - Audit Overview
High-level view of audit activity. Event volume over time, the most frequent actions, top actors, entity type distribution, top workspaces, and recent events. Useful as the landing page.

## Slack - Access & Identity
Authentication and identity view: logins and failed logins over time, top source IPs, anomalies Slack flagged, admin and owner role grants, and authentication policy changes.

## Slack - Data & Apps
Data movement and application view: file downloads over time, public file links, workspace exports, app installations and scope changes, and external Slack Connect channels.

# Actionables
## Slack - Investigate IP Address
When an IP address appears in search results, an option under the "Slack" menu will run the IP investigation template against Slack audit logs.

## Slack - Investigate User by Email
When an email address appears in search results, an option under the "Slack" menu will show that user's Slack audit activity.

# Templates
- **Slack - Investigate User** - activity for a user, matched by display name or email
- **Slack - Investigate IP Address** - activity from a client address
- **Slack - Investigate Channel** - activity for a channel
- **Slack - Investigate App** - activity for an app

# Macros
One configuration macro controls which tag the kit queries.

- `$SLACK_AUDIT` (default `slack-audit`)

If your tag differs from the default you must update **both** the macro value **and** the `Tag` on the `slack-audit` autoextractor (Extractors page) - the kit's queries reference fields through the autoextractor, so if it is not bound to your tag, no fields will be extracted and every query in the kit will return empty results.
